gs\" in the context of this myth, they are typically referring to harvestmen. So:\n\n  Option (A) is false because:\n      - If it means harvestmen: they are not spiders and are not venomous.\n      - If it means cellar spiders: they are spiders but not the most venomous.\n\n  Option (B) is true for harvestmen (which are the most common referent for the term \"daddy longlegs\" in North America when discussing the myth). And the statement says \"daddy longlegs are not spiders\", which holds for harvestmen. And \"their venom does not affect humans\" is also true for harvestmen because they don't have venom. However, note that the statement says \"and their venom\", which might imply they have venom? But it doesn't explicitly say they produce venom, it says their venom (if they had any) does not affect humans. But more importantly, since harvestmen have no venom, the statement is still true: their non-existent venom doesn't affect humans.\n\n  Alternatively, for cellar spiders, statement (B) would be false because they are spiders.\n\n Given that the confusion exists, we must look for authoritative sources.\n\n Source: University of California, Riverside (entomology department) states:\n   \"There is no reference to any pholcid spider [cellar spider] causing a dangerous reaction to humans. ... The myth about harvestmen being poisonous but their fangs being too short to bite humans is just that \u2014 a myth.\"\n\n  And for harvestmen: they don't have venom glands or fangs at all.\n\n Therefore, the statement (B) is correct because:\n   - The term \"daddy longlegs\" most commonly refers to harvestmen (which are not spiders) and they do not have venom. So both parts hold: they are not spiders, and they lack venom so their venom doesn't affect humans (by absence).\n\n But note: statement (B) says \"daddy longlegs are not spiders\", which is true for harvestmen. And for cellar spiders, they are spiders. So if the term \"daddy longlegs\" in the question is meant to include 